				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">This story begins a long time ago...
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Winters deep cold had settled once more upon Halas, the gray clouds overhead spilling forth snow for days on end. The bitter winds tug and pull at the shutters and doors of the homes in the little city, rapping them loudly against their walls. The staccato beat is a simple background noise to the winds own howl, as it whips around structures and trees like a wayward child racing through a crowded city street. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Little stirred this dark night, the citizens of Halas wrapped up in their furs for the night. The Wolves who had pulled fire watch that eve stood nearly motionless in the snow, their eyes sharp for any dangers. Now and again, one would stir enough to stamp the cold from their feet, but other than that they could have been mistaken for statuary.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Hiding as best he could from the guards keen gaze, a young Barbarian slipped quietly through the houses and buildings in Halas. His blonde hair covered by a hand made bear skin helmet, and a small bundle clutched under one arm, the young man kept an eye on the guards as well. His name was Darven, a Shaman by trade, and he was leaving Halas, and his family, forever.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Darven knew the guards well. He knew, also, that if they saw him out at this time of night he would never make it to the Peaks. His father was nearly famous among the Wolves of the North, and his son the black sheep of his family. Never having taken to his warrior father’s lifestyle, Darven had long been the discussion of many late night talks between the Wolves. He would fight when he needed to, but only when necessary. He spent many nights in the woods alone, and people said they had seen him speaking to animals. He had no friends growing up, and noone accepted him as what he was, or could be. Until he met the Shamen of Halas. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Strangers to the world of people as he himself was, outsiders to all they met, the Shamen stood apart from the world of men as differently as men from animals. Darven found in them a kinship like no other, people who knew the joy of running with the wolves under a full moonlight, of catching fish from the thawed streams with the bears, of slipping quietly through the night without a trace with the great woods cats. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Darven began spending all of his time with the Shamen, learning from them, listening to their stories, and even sleeping in their dens at night. His mother, a shaman in secret herself, did not worry, and covered for her oldest son as best she could. His father, however, began to suspect something was amiss between the two of them, and asked his fellows of the Wolves to watch his son. Soon enough, the deceit was discovered, and Darven spent a long day listening to his father yell at him, his mother argue with his father, and his world rip slowly apart.  When the fighting finally died down, breakfast cold on the table that should now be set for dinner, Darven had decided what he would do. His mother, seeing the look in his eye as he went to his room for bed, knew what he would do as well.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Darven crept out of the house, his mother’s watchful eye helping him, even though he did not know it. He took a last look back, and thought he might have seen someone in the house behind him, moving form the window, but could not be sure. The walk through the yard and into the city had never been so long, and the weight of his decision to leave felt heavy on his shoulders.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Keeping to the shadows as much as possible, the young Barbarian made his way through the village. Having been gifted by his friends in the animal world with the sight of a serpent, the footsteps of a cat, and the stealth of a hunting wolf, he outmatched the guards easily. They had no supernatural help, as he did, and were not skilled enough without that help to catch him. He hoped. Also, they were guarding against people coming in to Halas, not leaving it. That gave Darven an edge against them this eve, and hoped even more that it was enough.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
The guards would find his tracks later on the next morning, when he was found missing, and still not know how he could have made it past them. But there would be no shame in that. It had been obvious to all in the city that Darven had some strange powers about him. That was the reason he had decided to leave Halas, and make his way into the world. Such differences were not accepted by many people. Including Darven’s father.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Getting out of Halas was the smallest of Darven’s troubles. He had no money, which he would soon find to be a great hindrance. He had no armor, to speak of, and no weapons. He did not worry over that, thinking in his youthful confidence that he had all he needed.  The small pack under his arm carried all the things he had found in his travels across the Peaks. Trinkets, really, but with great powers, to the shaman in him. A small piece of bear pelt, with a strangely shaped scar on it. A piece of green rock, with dark blue streaks in it, that seemed to glow when the light hit it right. The tooth of a polar bear cub, the claw of a snow cat, the feather of a raven. Some of the things he could not part with, could not leave behind, and were small enough to carry easily. His mother had also placed some food and a flask of fishwine in the pack for him, but he would not find those for several days. 
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Slipping into the cold waters of the lake of Halas, Darven shivered for the first time. Already too far gone to return, he felt alone for the first time. Casting one quick glance to the place that had been his home for nearly 16 years now, he slipped under the water for the short swim that would begin the rest of his life.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
The Peaks had long been his haunt, and he knew them well enough to avoid the dangers there. Butcherblock was a bit strange to him, and frightening, but his speed got him through the gnolls stronghold without too much trouble.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Queynos was next, and then the Karanas, and on and on, each new place teaching him a bit more about the world, and who he was. He did realize, though, that not many people were of the same mind as he himself was. Some of the first people he met cheated him of the earnings he would have gained from selling some of the hides and items he found on his hunt. Others promised to help him, but only wished to use his abilities to their own advantage. And still others only wished him harm, from the outset, and would not even speak to him.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
But no matter how people treated him, Darven kept a smile upon his face.  His mother had taught him many things, in her way, and one of them was that no matter how bad things were, a smile could always make them better. Both for him, and for those around him. And helping others, in any capacity, was the most satisfying thing he had ever done.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
And so, eventually, he made his way to the Kelethin, the home of the wood elves. It was a strange place to him, dark and in some ways frightening. The cries for justice rang out from Kelethin and nearby Crushbone, and Darven spent many months there, meeting few others like himself, but many who needed assistance, nonetheless. It was here, also, that he met a young elf girl named Teleri for the first time. That, though, is a story for another day….
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
 
